{
  "case_id": "2022-21-1449",
  "document_hash": "58da32d145519c9ceb45b3442a1ab6015b449e98e293c6c6483c2ec2ea5db525",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1449",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58da32d145519c9ceb45b3442a1ab6015b449e98e293c6c6483c2ec2ea5db525",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58da32d14551-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:05 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1449",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58da32d145519c9ceb45b3442a1ab6015b449e98e293c6c6483c2ec2ea5db525",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58da32d14551-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Court and the Board have long recognized that the intentional destruction of an employer's property in the course of a labor dispute is not protected concerted activity."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1449",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58da32d145519c9ceb45b3442a1ab6015b449e98e293c6c6483c2ec2ea5db525",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58da32d14551-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: The SG suggests that after a hiatus, a jurisdictional hiatus, you could pursue your claims. Why isn't that adequate?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1449",
      "confidence": 0.96,
      "document_hash": "58da32d145519c9ceb45b3442a1ab6015b449e98e293c6c6483c2ec2ea5db525",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58da32d14551-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Thank you, counsel."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1449",
      "confidence": 0.94,
      "document_hash": "58da32d145519c9ceb45b3442a1ab6015b449e98e293c6c6483c2ec2ea5db525",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58da32d14551-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 35,
        "start": 35
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think this might be related, but when you were talking to Justice Thomas, you were in the middle of a sentence, and I was hanging on every word. And I want you to finish the sentence, if you can remember it, because you said something like now, look, the state court can get it wrong. So --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1449",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58da32d145519c9ceb45b3442a1ab6015b449e98e293c6c6483c2ec2ea5db525",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58da32d14551-0002-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the first and most important legal reason is this Court's decision in Longshoremen against Davis. In that case, the Court said that Garmon preemption is established when the party asserting preemption presents evidence from which the Board could reasonably conclude that the conduct is protected."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1449",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58da32d145519c9ceb45b3442a1ab6015b449e98e293c6c6483c2ec2ea5db525",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58da32d14551-0002-0004",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the closest analogy I've been able to find is with the Interstate Commerce Commission. The Court developed the doctrine of primary jurisdiction, which required state courts under some circumstances to await the Interstate Commerce Commission's decisions as to whether a particular rate is reasonable."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Jackson",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1449",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58da32d145519c9ceb45b3442a1ab6015b449e98e293c6c6483c2ec2ea5db525",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58da32d14551-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 68,
        "start": 68
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE JACKSON: What about the focus\non the intent, on the conspiratorial nature of\nthis, on the attempt to hurt the employee --\nthe -- the -- the -- as opposed to just the"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Jackson",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1449",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58da32d145519c9ceb45b3442a1ab6015b449e98e293c6c6483c2ec2ea5db525",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58da32d14551-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 87,
        "start": 87
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE JACKSON: What -- do you agree\nor disagree with the following statement? And\nthis is from opposing counsel's brief: \"When a\nunion deliberately orchestrates a scheme for\nthe very purpose of destroying an employer's\nproperty, there is no plausible argument that\nthis conduct is protected under the NLRA.\""
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1449",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58da32d145519c9ceb45b3442a1ab6015b449e98e293c6c6483c2ec2ea5db525",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58da32d14551-0003-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 87,
        "start": 87
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. DALMAT: I think it's an overbroad\nstatement because property could be anything.\nProperty could be goodwill. Property could be\nmoney. Property could be intangibles. And so,\nstated at that level of generality, it's simply\ntoo overbroad."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1449",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58da32d145519c9ceb45b3442a1ab6015b449e98e293c6c6483c2ec2ea5db525",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58da32d14551-0003-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 88,
        "start": 88
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "protected unless the strikers fail to take\nreasonable precautions to avoid aggravated,\nimminent, foreseeable harm to employer\nproperty. That's the correct test."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1449",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58da32d145519c9ceb45b3442a1ab6015b449e98e293c6c6483c2ec2ea5db525",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58da32d14551-0003-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 88,
        "start": 88
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the Board has never found spoilage of perishables to be aggravated harm."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1449",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58da32d145519c9ceb45b3442a1ab6015b449e98e293c6c6483c2ec2ea5db525",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58da32d14551-0003-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 88,
        "start": 88
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I have not found an NLRB case that has said a strike was wrong if it involved perishable products or the loss of perishable products."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1449",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58da32d145519c9ceb45b3442a1ab6015b449e98e293c6c6483c2ec2ea5db525",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58da32d14551-0004-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 90,
        "start": 90
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Under your test, if the driver simply discharges cement or stop the drums from rotating, would you agree with Petitioner?\nMR. DALMAT: Yes, Your Honor.\nJUSTICE THOMAS: Okay. Now you make the point about the judicial -- the jurisprudential hiatus, and as I've said before, these other terms, \"exhaustion\" and \"preemption,\" have come up. Could you give me your best textual basis for any of those, for either of those?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Noel J. Francisco (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1449",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58da32d145519c9ceb45b3442a1ab6015b449e98e293c6c6483c2ec2ea5db525",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58da32d14551-0004-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 92,
        "start": 92
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "First, this is not a case about the mere stoppage of work. Here, the union had the workers show up, accept possession of the concrete, begin deliveries of the concrete, abandon those deliveries when it was too late to save the concrete, and then countermand supervisor instructions to complete the deliveries that had already been started, which at that point in time was the only way to save the concrete.\nIt's really no different than the riverboat crew that drives out into the middle of the river and then abandons ship. That is not merely a stoppage of work."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1449",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58da32d145519c9ceb45b3442a1ab6015b449e98e293c6c6483c2ec2ea5db525",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58da32d14551-0004-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 96,
        "start": 96
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Thank you, counsel. The case is submitted.\n(Whereupon, at 11:31 a.m., the case was submitted.)"
    },
    {
